Build a complete, production-ready Admin Dashboard for the SJ Law Academy platform and integrate it into the existing React/Vite application without breaking the current student-facing website, routing, authentication, UI components, or existing functionality. The Admin Dashboard must be a secure, responsive, professional content-management and website-management system that allows authorized administrators and page managers to control the entire platform from one central interface. Create a dedicated protected `/admin` route with a modern dashboard layout containing a collapsible sidebar, top navigation/header, admin profile menu, notifications, global search, breadcrumbs, responsive mobile navigation, dashboard statistics, recent activity, quick actions, and role-based navigation. Implement a proper role and permission system with at least `Super Admin`, `Admin`, `Page Manager`, `Content Manager`, and `Support Manager` roles, where Super Admin has unrestricted access, Admin can manage most platform operations, Page Managers can edit and publish assigned website pages and content but cannot access sensitive settings, payments, users, or administrator management, Content Managers can create and manage academic content, and Support Managers can manage support tickets and notifications; permissions must be enforced on the backend/database and not merely hidden in the frontend. The dashboard should allow authorized users to manage all public website pages including Homepage, About, Academic Levels, Pricing, Contact, FAQ, Terms, Privacy Policy, and other future pages, with a visual page editor/content-management interface where managers can create, edit, preview, save drafts, publish, unpublish, schedule, duplicate, archive, and restore content, while maintaining version history and recording who made each change and when. Build a complete academic content management system allowing administrators to create, edit, delete, publish, and organize academic levels such as 100L, 200L, 300L, 400L, and 500L; courses; topics; lessons; text notes; lecture materials; videos; PDFs; downloadable resources; statutes; case law; dictionaries; practice questions; MCQs; past questions; problem questions; scenario questions; essay questions; mock examinations; and other learning materials, with fields for titles, descriptions, academic level, course, topic, tags, categories, difficulty, status, ordering, featured status, author, publication date, cover image, attachments, video URL, SEO information, and other appropriate metadata. Provide drag-and-drop or ordering controls where appropriate so administrators can control the order of academic levels, courses, topics, lessons, questions, resources, and homepage sections. Include a powerful media/file manager connected to Supabase Storage that allows authorized users to upload, preview, organize, rename, replace, download, and delete images, PDFs, videos, documents, thumbnails, logos, and other supported files, with file size/type validation and appropriate access controls; media should not be unnecessarily duplicated when reused across pages. Create a dedicated case-law management module where administrators can add and edit case names, citations, court, year, judges, facts, issues, judgment, legal principles, summary, related courses/topics, searchable keywords, attachments, and publication status. Create a practice and examination management module where administrators can build question banks, create question sets and mock examinations, define question types, answers, explanations, marks, difficulty, timing, academic level, course/topic association, ordering, and publication status, and allow questions to be reused across multiple exams without duplicating records. Create a student management section where authorized administrators can search, filter, view, edit, activate/deactivate, suspend, and manage student accounts, view academic levels, subscriptions, learning progress, practice/exam activity, bookmarks, notifications, payment history, and account status, while ensuring sensitive information is appropriately protected. Create a subscription and payment management section showing subscription plans, academic levels, active subscriptions, expired subscriptions, trial accounts, payment records, Paystack transaction references, payment status, amounts, dates, renewals, failed transactions, refunds where applicable, and subscription entitlements; administrators should be able to inspect transactions and subscription status but sensitive Paystack secret credentials must never be exposed in the frontend. Integrate payment management with Supabase Edge Functions and Paystack webhook data so that payment status and student entitlements are controlled by the backend rather than manually trusted frontend state. Create a notifications management system allowing authorized administrators to compose, preview, schedule, publish, and send platform notifications to all students, specific academic levels, specific courses, selected users, or other defined groups, with notification history and read/delivery status where supported. Create a support/help management section for FAQs, support tickets, contact enquiries, categories, ticket status, priority, assigned support manager, internal notes, responses, and resolution history. Add a global search system across users, courses, topics, lessons, case law, questions, resources, subscriptions, payments, pages, and media, with filters, sorting, pagination, and appropriate search indexes. Add an analytics dashboard showing total registered students, active students, trial users, active subscriptions, expired subscriptions, revenue/payment statistics, course enrollment/access statistics, learning activity, completed topics, practice attempts, examination attempts, popular courses, popular content, recent registrations, recent payments, recent content updates, and other useful platform metrics; use charts and tables where appropriate and ensure analytics are calculated from real database data. Add a complete audit-log system recording important administrative actions such as login, logout, page creation, page editing, publishing, deletion, user changes, subscription changes, content uploads, media deletion, payment-related administrative actions, and role/permission changes, including the acting user, action, affected record, timestamp, and relevant metadata. Add a dedicated Admin Users and Roles section where Super Admins can create administrator/page-manager accounts, assign roles, assign specific permissions, activate/deactivate administrators, reset access where appropriate, and restrict Page Managers to specific pages, content categories, academic levels, or modules. Build a proper dashboard home showing useful statistics and actionable information rather than placeholder cards, including pending drafts, unpublished content, recent activity, support tickets, failed payments, expiring subscriptions, recently registered students, and quick actions such as Create Course, Upload Resource, Add Case Law, Create Question, Edit Homepage, Manage Students, and View Payments. Implement autosave or draft-saving where appropriate, confirmation dialogs for destructive actions, unsaved-change warnings, loading states, empty states, skeleton states, success/error notifications, validation messages, pagination, filtering, bulk actions, and clear permission-denied screens. The content editor should support rich text formatting while sanitizing user-generated HTML to prevent XSS and other security vulnerabilities. Use Supabase PostgreSQL as the persistent database, Supabase Auth for administrator authentication, Supabase Storage for media/files, Row Level Security policies for database authorization, and Supabase Edge Functions for privileged server-side operations such as payment processing, webhooks, email/notification operations, and other operations that must not expose secrets to the browser. Do not hard-code administrator credentials, permissions, payment keys, or sensitive configuration in the frontend. Protect every admin route and every sensitive database operation with server-side authorization. Make the dashboard fully responsive for desktop, tablet, and mobile, but optimize the desktop experience for administrative work. Maintain the existing SJ Law Academy visual identity while making the admin interface clearly distinct from the student-facing interface. Organize the code into maintainable reusable components and modules rather than putting the entire dashboard into one file. Create appropriate database schemas, relationships, indexes, RLS policies, storage buckets, authentication logic, API/Edge Function interfaces, TypeScript types, validation schemas, and frontend services required for the system to work end-to-end. Replace mock administrative data with real Supabase data wherever functionality has already been implemented. Ensure that deleting content does not accidentally break references to courses, topics, questions, resources, bookmarks, progress, or student records; use appropriate foreign-key relationships, soft deletion, archival, or dependency warnings where necessary. Implement SEO controls for public pages where relevant, including page title, meta description, canonical URL, social image, indexing status, and structured metadata fields. Include preview functionality so administrators and Page Managers can preview unpublished page/content changes before publishing. Include publishing workflows such as Draft → Review → Published → Unpublished/Archived, with permissions determining who can create, review, publish, or delete content. Ensure all timestamps are stored consistently and displayed appropriately. The finished result should be a real working CMS/admin management system rather than a static visual mockup: every major button, form, upload control, table action, filter, editor, publishing action, role assignment, user-management action, and dashboard statistic should be connected to the appropriate backend/database functionality. Before making changes, inspect the existing project structure and reuse existing components, types, styling conventions, authentication architecture, and route patterns where appropriate; do not unnecessarily rewrite working student-facing functionality. Also identify and resolve existing routing inconsistencies such as `/levels` versus `/academic-levels`, `/learn/...` versus `/courses/.../topic/...`, duplicate legacy page implementations, and other conflicting navigation structures where they affect the new admin system. At the end, provide a clear implementation summary showing every new admin route, database table, storage bucket, role and permission, Edge Function, major component, and integration created, along with any existing project functionality that still requires implementation.
